  "heading": "\\greatest.common.divisor:of{a, b}",
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{greatest common divisor of }a\\textrm{ and }b\\quad\\htmlClass{mlg-title-written}{\\gcd(a, b)}",
  "body_text": null,
  "page": null,
  "source": "[\\greatest.common.divisor:of{a, b}]\nDefines: d is \\integer\nwhen: a, b is \\integer\nexpresses:\n. if:\n  . anyOf:\n    . a != \\integer.zero\n    . b != \\integer.zero\n  then:\n  . \\integer.zero \\:integer.less?:/ d\n  . d \\.integer.divides./ a\n  . d \\.integer.divides./ b\n  . forAll: c is \\integer\n    where:\n    . c \\.integer.divides./ a\n    . c \\.integer.divides./ b\n    then: c \\.integer.divides./ d\n. if:\n  . a = \\integer.zero\n  . b = \\integer.zero\n  then: d = \\integer.zero\n. exists: x, y is \\integer\n  suchThat: d = (a \\.integer.*./ x) \\.integer.+./ (b \\.integer.*./ y)\nDocumented:\n. called: \"greatest common divisor of $a?$ and $b?$\"\n. written: \"\\gcd(a?, b?)\"\n. description: \"For a pair not both zero, the greatest common divisor is the\n                unique positive common divisor divisible by every common\n                divisor. Bezout's identity is included in the characterization;\n                the definition is totalized by the convention $\\gcd(0,0)=0$.\"\nId: \"3f6acf7f-8ea8-4080-a9bc-23cddc72bdd5\"",
